A
check valve
is a simple yet essential component in hydraulic systems, designed to allow fluid flow in
one direction
while
completely blocking
flow in the opposite direction. Because of this function, it is often referred to as a
one-way directional control valve
.
Despite their simplicity, check valves play a crucial role in many hydraulic applications. They ensure the proper operation of pumps, prevent system damage, and contribute to the overall efficiency of fluid power circuits. Some of the most common applications of check valves include:
Holding Prime
– In many hydraulic systems, especially those using
larger pumps
, oil can drain from the
inlet line
when the system is idle for extended periods. This condition, known as
losing prime
, occurs when the pump struggles to draw oil back into the system because atmospheric pressure alone is insufficient to push the fluid up the supply tube. A
check valve
installed in the inlet line prevents oil from draining out, ensuring the pump maintains its prime and is ready for operation when restarted.
Avoiding Pump Reversal
– In some hydraulic circuits, when the electric motor driving the pump is turned off,
fluid pressure in the system can force oil backward through the pump
. Many pumps are not designed to rotate in reverse and can suffer severe damage if this occurs. To prevent reverse flow, a check valve is placed at the
pump outlet
, allowing flow to move forward while blocking any reverse movement.
Bypass Flow
– Check valves are often used to create
bypass pathways
around specific components in a circuit. For example, a check valve may be placed in parallel with a
flow control valve
to regulate flow in one direction while allowing free flow in the other. This setup ensures controlled operation in one direction while
minimizing restriction
in the reverse direction.
Creating Back Pressure
– In some
directional control valves (DCVs)
, oil pressure is required to shift the spool. Certain valve designs require a
minimum system pressure
to ensure proper shifting. A check valve can be installed in the
tank line
to create
back pressure
, preventing pressure from dropping too low and ensuring reliable valve operation.
Beyond these applications, check valves are also used to
lock actuators in place
and provide
free-flow pathways for hydraulic motors during deceleration
. These functions will be explored in greater detail in later chapters.